## Final Judgement
### Summary of Key Findings
The fact-check reveals no evidence that the Indian government or the BJP ever announced, implemented, or referenced a policy that conditioned the free distribution of COVID-19 vaccines on an individual’s voting record or political affiliation. Multiple sources, including official government announcements and news reports from October 2020 onward, consistently show that the vaccination program was based on health-risk prioritization (e.g., healthcare workers, the elderly) and was framed as a public health initiative available universally at no cost at government centers. Even during election campaigns, promises of free vaccines were pitched as party pledges if elected, not as benefits tied to individual voting behavior.

### Remaining Information
No critical information gaps remain. The evidence from government sources, health ministry FAQs, news articles, and descriptions of the Co-WIN platform uniformly confirms that vaccine eligibility and access were defined by medical and logistical criteria, not by electoral participation.

### Conclusion
Based on the recorded evidence, the claim that people who did not vote for the BJP in 2020 would be denied free COVID-19 vaccines is not supported by any official policy, statement, or implementation. In fact, the government’s vaccination plan explicitly based free distribution on priority health groups, with no mention of voting conditions. The claim appears to misrepresent a political campaign promise (free vaccines if BJP wins in Bihar) as a discriminatory policy tied to individual votes. Therefore, the claim is refuted. `refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check finds no evidence supporting the claim; investigations show that free COVID-19 vaccine distribution in India was based on health-risk prioritization (e.g., healthcare workers, the elderly) under a [special immunization program](https://m.economictimes.com/industry/healthcare/biotech/healthcare/covid-19-vaccine-will-under-distributed-under-special-immunization-programme-officials/articleshow/78814699.cms), with no conditions linked to voting behavior. Official government policy and the [Co-WIN platform](https://www.mohfw.gov.in/pdf/FAQsCOVID19vaccinesvaccinationprogramWebsiteupload.pdf) specified eligibility by medical criteria, not political affiliation, and even during the 2020 elections, [party pledges](https://www.newsclick.in/Bihar-Elections-BJP-Promises-Free-COVID-19-Vaccines-Opposition-%23-VaccinePolitics) were not implemented as vote-based policies. Therefore, the claim is refuted.